Let $(X,M,*)$ be a fuzzy metric space in the sense of George and Veeramani: $X$ is a nonempty set, $*$ is a continuous t-norm, and $M:X\times X\times(0,\infty)\to(0,1]$ satisfies, for all $x,y,z\in X$ and $t,s>0$:
(i) $M(x,y,t)>0$;
(ii) $M(x,y,t)=1$ if and only if $x=y$;
(iii) $M(x,y,t)=M(y,x,t)$;
(iv) $M(x,y,t)*M(y,z,s)\le M(x,z,t+s)$;
(v) $M(x,y,\cdot):(0,\infty)\to(0,1]$ is continuous.
The fuzzy metric $M$ induces a topology on $X$ for which the family of open balls $B_M(x,r,t)=\{y\in X: M(x,y,t)>1-r\}$, $r\in(0,1)$, $t>0$, is a base. Call $(X,M,*)$ compact if this induced topology is compact, and finite if $X$ is a finite set.

Two compact fuzzy metric spaces $(X,M,*)$ and $(Y,N,*)$ are isometric if there is a bijection $\varphi:X\to Y$ with $N(\varphi(x),\varphi(x'),t)=M(x,x',t)$ for all $x,x'\in X$, $t>0$. Let $\mathrm{CFM}$ denote the collection of isometry classes of compact fuzzy metric spaces equipped with a fixed continuous t-norm $*$, endowed with the fuzzy Gromov–Hausdorff metric (the natural fuzzy analogue of the classical Gromov–Hausdorff distance, defined via infima of fuzzy Hausdorff distances over all isometric embeddings of two given compact fuzzy metric spaces into a common ambient fuzzy metric space).

Question: Is the set of (isometry classes of) finite fuzzy metric spaces dense in $\mathrm{CFM}$ with respect to the fuzzy Gromov–Hausdorff metric?

